# Go Architecture Rules

## Clean Architecture
- Separate concerns: controllers, services, repositories
- Use dependency injection for all dependencies
- Interface-based design for testability

## Repository Pattern
- All database operations go through repository interfaces
- Repositories are defined in `internal/db/` with interfaces in `IDbContext.go`
- Controllers use repositories, never direct database access
- Follow the pattern: `UserRepository`, `BlogpostRepository`, etc.

## Dependency Injection
- Use the DI container in `internal/di/container.go`
- Register all dependencies in the container
- Controllers receive dependencies through constructor injection

## Package Structure
- `cmd/` - Application entry point
- `internal/` - Private application code
- `internal/controllers/` - HTTP handlers and business logic
- `internal/db/` - Database layer and repositories
- `internal/models/` - Database models
- `internal/dto/` - Data transfer objects
- `internal/middleware/` - HTTP middleware
- `internal/config/` - Configuration management
- `internal/logger/` - Logging utilities
- `internal/di/` - Dependency injection
- `migrations/` - Database migrations (Flyway)

## Naming Conventions
- Use PascalCase for exported types and functions
- Use camelCase for variables and unexported functions
- Repository interfaces: `IUserRepository`, `IBlogpostRepository`
- Repository implementations: `UserRepository`, `BlogpostRepository`
- Controllers: `AuthController`, `BlogController`, `ShopController`
- Services: `AuthService`, `BlogService`
